>
  <p>
    Use this template when the post has runnable code. Inline references like
    <code>torch.nn.Linear</code> or <code>--num-workers=8</code> appear inside
    <code>&lt;code&gt;</code>; multi-line snippets go in fenced blocks.
  </p>

  <h2>Setup</h2>

  <p>
    Before the code, state the dependency surface clearly:
  </p>

  <pre><code>pip install torch==2.2 transformers==4.41 accelerate</code></pre>

  <p>
    A short reproducibility note: pin both the framework and the model. Posts that
    say "I used PyTorch and Transformers" are not reproducible.
  </p>

  <h2>The core idea</h2>

  <p>
    The fewer lines, the better the demonstration. Strip imports and boilerplate
    until only the load-bearing code remains:
  </p>

  <pre><code>import torch
from transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer

model_id = "Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct"
tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_id)
model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(
    model_id, torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16, device_map="auto"
)

prompt = "Explain BM25 in one sentence."
ids = tok(prompt, return_tensors="pt").to(model.device)
with torch.inference_mode():
    out = model.generate(**ids, max_new_tokens=64, temperature=0.0)
print(tok.decode(out[0][ids.input_ids.shape[1]:], skip_special_tokens=True))</code></pre>

  <p>
    Annotate inline if a line is non-obvious. Long comment blocks belong in real
    code, not blog snippets — the post itself is the comment.
  </p>

  <h2>What changed and why</h2>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Pinned dtype.</strong> Implicit fp32 on Hopper costs 2× memory.</li>
    <li><strong>Single-token decode.</strong> Greedy here so the example is deterministic.</li>
    <li><strong>Slice the prompt out.</strong> Otherwise the printed output includes the question.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout">
    <p class="callout-title">Note</p>
    <p>Callouts pull a single sentence out of the flow. Use them sparingly — every callout dilutes every other callout.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Performance numbers</h2>

  <p>
    Always include a measurement. "It works" is not a finding; "it works in 230 ms
    on an H100, vs. 410 ms before" is.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <p class="footnote">
    Footnote: tested on H100 80GB, CUDA 12.4, transformers 4.41.2.
  </p>
